INT. RETROFITTED SCHOOL BUS BRIDAL SHOP - DAY

A yellow school bus gutted and rebuilt as a boutique: tulle hung from the luggage racks, chandeliers swaying from the ceiling, a three-way mirror bolted where the driver's seat used to be. Sunlight falls through the stop-sign window in a red octagon on the floor.

PHILOMENA TRASK (30s, bride, hair half-pinned, bare feet) stands on a milk crate in a billowing gown. GWENDOLYN OSEI-MARTINSEN (60s, shop owner, pins in her mouth, measuring tape like a stole) kneels at the hem. LUCIEN FAURE (30s, best man, tuxedo jacket over jeans) lounges on a bench seat eating a muffin. IMANI REYNOLDS (20s, maid of honor, phone in hand) paces the aisle. BARTHOLOMEW FINCH (70s, bus driver, still in uniform, newspaper) sits at the wheel, pretending to read.

GWENDOLYN
(around the pins)
Arms up.

PHILOMENA
They are up.

GWENDOLYN
Up like you mean it.

Philomena raises her arms. The dress gapes. Imani gasps.

IMANI
It's too big!

LUCIEN
(mouth full)
It's perfect.

Imani and Philomena speak together.

IMANI
It's not perfect!

PHILOMENA
^I love it!

They stop. Look at each other. Imani's phone rings. She answers, thumb flying.

AUNT CORDELIA (V.O.)
(over speaker, crackling)
Is she in the dress? I want a photo. Don't let her cry. She'll cry.

PHILOMENA
I'm not crying.

AUNT CORDELIA (V.O.)
Is she crying?

IMANI
A little.

PHILOMENA
I'm not!

She is. Gwendolyn stands, spits the pins into her palm, and wipes the bride's cheek with the measuring tape.

GWENDOLYN
Hush, child.

Bartholomew lowers the newspaper.

BARTHOLOMEW
Are we leaving, or what?

GWENDOLYN
(to the room)
We are fitting a bride!

BARTHOLOMEW
(to his paper)
Fitting a bride in a bus. My mother would never.

Lucien licks muffin from his thumb.

LUCIEN
Phil, honestly. You look like a lighthouse.

PHILOMENA
That's not a compliment.

LUCIEN
It is where I'm from.

Everything seems fine. The chandeliers tinkle. The sun burns the floor. Philomena steps off the crate and walks the aisle, the gown dragging behind her like a wake.

Then the bus lurches. The engine coughs and catches. Bartholomew is leaning on the wheel, and one foot is flat on the accelerator.

BARTHOLOMEW
(with great calm)
Hold on.

Everyone stumbles. The bus rolls forward. Gwendolyn grabs a rack. Imani drops the phone. Lucien's muffin lands in Philomena's veil. The bus rolls through the garage doors and into the street, a wedding on wheels, tulle streaming out the windows like a flag.

AUNT CORDELIA (V.O.)
(from the phone, on the floor)
Hello? Is the bus moving?
